  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 400 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nAfter coming off a hard freeze Thursday night, temperatures will \ngradually warm up Friday after melting off frost and potentially \nsnow for some areas. As the Pacific storm exists towards the \nGreat Lakes region, upper-level ridging will begin to build in \nbehind it. The ridge will rapidly usher in warm air aloft, with \n700 mb temperatures rising into the upper single digits. Despite\nthe warm temperatures aloft, surface temperatures will take \nlonger to rebound. Highs on Friday will be about 10 degrees \nbelow average, with temperatures generally in the 50s across the\nforecast area. Areas with snow cover could be cooler, namely \nthe Laramie Valley, with highs in the 40s. Even though \ntemperatures will be on the seasonably cool side, ample sunshine\nthroughout the day will help cut through some of the chill.\n\nAs the upper-level ridge shifts eastward overnight on Friday, mid-\nlevel winds will become more westerly. The westerly winds will pose \na possible concern for high winds in the typical southeast Wyoming \nwind prones of Arlington and Bordeaux during the day Saturday. MSLP \ngradients will steepen along and west of the Laramie Range. CAG to \nCPR 850 mb gradients also reach the 50 meter range, signaling \nthe potential for gradient driven marginal high winds in the \nwind prones. 700 mb winds will reach about 40 kts, but \nsubsidence is rather weak and will likely not play a huge role \nin the winds Saturday into Sunday morning. Wind gusts of at \nleast 40 to 50 MPH will be likely during the day Saturday and \ninto the overnight hours. Westerly winds will help play a role \nin the warm, above average temperatures on Saturday. Downsloping\nwinds will aid 90th percentile 700 mb temperatures in achieving\ndaytime highs in the 70s for areas east of the Laramie Range. \nTo the west, highs around 60 degrees can be expected. Ample \nsunshine is once again expected for Saturday.\n\nAbove average temperatures will continue into the start of next week \nwith highs in the 60s and 70s expected Sunday through Tuesday. \nConditions during this time are also expected to remain dry, with \nmostly sunny skies expected each day. Cannot rule out a low-end \nchance of precipitation during the evening on Tuesday as a shortwave \nglances the CWA, however, models seem to have trended this\ndrier.",
